    'subject': 'Government',
    'chapterTitle': 'Elements of a State',
    'body': 'A state is a politically organised body of people, permanently occupying a definite territory, independent of external control, and possessing a government with the authority and capacity to make and enforce laws over all persons and property within its boundaries. For a political entity to be recognised as a state, it must possess four essential elements: population, territory, government and sovereignty. The absence of any one of these elements means the entity cannot properly be regarded as a state in the full political sense.\n\nPopulation refers to the total number of people, both citizens and permanent residents, who inhabit the territory of a state at any given time. While there is no fixed minimum or maximum population required for statehood, a viable state generally needs a population large enough to support its economic activities, defend its territory, and sustain its government institutions, though there is no universally agreed figure, as evidenced by both very small states such as Vatican City and very large states such as China and India, both of which are recognised as sovereign states despite their vastly different population sizes.\n\nTerritory refers to the definite geographical area, including land, water bodies such as rivers and lakes, and airspace, over which a state exercises exclusive control and authority. A state must have clearly defined and recognised boundaries, though these boundaries need not be entirely free from all dispute, as many states have unresolved border disagreements with neighbouring states yet remain fully recognised as sovereign entities. Territory is essential because it establishes the physical jurisdiction within which the laws and authority of the state apply and are enforced.\n\nGovernment refers to the organised political institution or machinery through which the state exercises its authority, formulates and implements policies, makes and enforces laws, and manages the affairs of the population within its territory. Every state must possess an organised government, regardless of its particular form, whether democratic, monarchical, military or otherwise, because it is through government that the abstract concept of the state becomes an active, functioning political reality capable of exercising authority over its population and territory.\n\nSovereignty is perhaps the most crucial and defining element of statehood, referring to the supreme, final and absolute authority of a state to govern itself without external interference or control, both internally (internal sovereignty, the supreme authority over all persons and institutions within the state territory) and externally (external sovereignty, also called independence, the freedom of the state from control or interference by any other external state or power in the conduct of its own affairs). A state that lacks external sovereignty, such as a colony or a protectorate under the control of another state, cannot be regarded as a fully sovereign state until it attains genuine independence, as Nigeria did from British colonial rule on 1 October 1960.\n\nBeyond these four essential elements, a state is also generally expected to gain recognition, which is the formal acknowledgment by other existing states that a newly formed political entity possesses the necessary qualifications of statehood and is therefore entitled to be treated as a full and equal member of the international community. Recognition may be granted individually by other states or collectively through admission into international organisations such as the United Nations, though scholars debate whether recognition is a strictly necessary fifth element of statehood or merely a practical consequence that naturally follows once the four essential elements are genuinely present.\n\nSample Exam-Style Questions:\n1. Question: State and explain the four essential elements of a state. Answer: The four essential elements are population, which is the people inhabiting the state; territory, which is the definite geographical area over which the state exercises authority; government, which is the organised institution through which the state exercises its authority; and sovereignty, which is the supreme authority of the state to govern itself free from external control.\n2. Question: Distinguish between internal sovereignty and external sovereignty. Answer: Internal sovereignty refers to the supreme authority of the state over all persons, groups and institutions within its own territory, meaning no other internal body has authority above that of the state. External sovereignty, also called independence, refers to the freedom of the state from control or interference by any other state or external power in the conduct of its own domestic and foreign affairs.
3. Question: Toluwani argues that a large population is essential for a territory to be recognised as a state. Using appropriate examples, evaluate this claim. Answer: Toluwani's claim is not entirely accurate. While a population is indeed one of the essential elements of statehood, there is no minimum population threshold that must be met. Vatican City, for example, has an extremely small population yet is recognised as a sovereign state, while countries such as China and India have very large populations and are equally recognised as sovereign states. What matters is the presence of an organised population, not any particular size.
4. Question: Explain why a colony, despite having a population, defined territory and even a local administrative government, is not regarded as a true state. Answer: A colony lacks external sovereignty, since it remains under the ultimate political control and authority of a colonising foreign power, which retains the final say over the colony major affairs, including foreign policy and defence. Without this independence from external control, the colony cannot be regarded as a fully sovereign state, regardless of the other elements it may possess.\n\nRevision and Summary Points:\n- A state is a politically organised body of people occupying a definite territory.\n- The four essential elements of a state are population, territory, government and sovereignty.\n- Population refers to the people inhabiting a state territory.\n- There is no fixed minimum population required for statehood.\n- Territory is the defined geographical area under a state authority.\n- Territory includes land, water bodies and airspace.\n- A state must have recognised, though not necessarily fully undisputed, boundaries.\n- Government is the institution through which a state exercises authority.\n- Every state must have some form of organised government.\n- Sovereignty is the supreme and final authority of a state.\n- Internal sovereignty is supreme authority over persons within the state.\n- External sovereignty is freedom from control by other states, also called independence.\n- A colony lacks external sovereignty and is therefore not a true state.\n- Nigeria gained independence from Britain on 1 October 1960.\n- Recognition is the acknowledgment of a state by other states.\n- Recognition may be individual or collective through bodies like the United Nations.\n- Scholars debate whether recognition is a necessary fifth element of statehood.\n- Vatican City is an example of a very small but sovereign state.\n- China and India are examples of very large sovereign states.\n- A state remains a state even during changes of government.\n- Loss of any one essential element can threaten a state continued existence.\n- Territory establishes the jurisdiction within which state laws apply.
- Sovereignty distinguishes a full state from a mere administrative region.\n- International law governs relations and recognition between sovereign states.\n- A state is distinct from a nation, which refers to a group with common cultural identity.\n- A nation-state occurs when a nation and a state largely coincide.\n- Federal states like Nigeria have multiple levels of government within one sovereign state.\n- The concept of statehood is fundamental to the study of Government and international relations.\n- Understanding the elements of a state helps explain international disputes and recognition issues.\n- Every citizen indirectly benefits from the presence of a functioning sovereign state.',
