    'subject': 'Government',
    'chapterTitle': 'Organs of Government: Legislature, Executive and Judiciary',
    'body': 'In virtually every modern state, the functions of government are divided among three principal organs, each responsible for a distinct aspect of governmental activity, namely the legislature, the executive and the judiciary. This tripartite division of governmental functions forms the structural foundation upon which the doctrine of separation of powers, discussed in detail as a related topic, is built, and understanding the distinct roles and composition of each organ is essential to understanding how modern government actually operates.\n\nThe legislature is the organ of government primarily responsible for making laws for the state, and it may also be referred to as the parliament, the National Assembly (as in Nigeria), Congress (as in the United States), or the House of Commons and House of Lords (as in the United Kingdom). Legislatures may be structured as unicameral, consisting of a single chamber, or bicameral, consisting of two chambers, typically a lower house (such as the House of Representatives in Nigeria, usually elected on the basis of population, with members representing smaller constituencies) and an upper house (such as the Senate in Nigeria, typically representing larger geographical or political units such as states, often with an equal number of representatives per unit regardless of population). Beyond its primary law-making function, the legislature also performs other important functions, including representing the interests and views of the citizens who elected its members, approving the national budget and controlling public expenditure through its power of the purse, exercising oversight over the activities of the executive arm to ensure accountability and prevent abuse of power, ratifying treaties and important international agreements entered into by the executive, and in some systems, having the power to remove certain executive or judicial officials through processes such as impeachment.\n\nThe executive is the organ of government primarily responsible for implementing, administering and enforcing the laws made by the legislature, and for managing the day to day affairs of the state. The executive is typically headed by a President (in a presidential system, as in Nigeria and the United States) or a Prime Minister (in a parliamentary system, as in the United Kingdom), and includes various ministers, departments, agencies and the civil service that carry out the practical work of government administration. The executive can be broadly divided into the political executive, comprising elected or appointed officials such as the President, Vice President and Ministers, who determine government policy, and the permanent (career) executive, comprising civil servants and bureaucrats who remain in their positions regardless of changes in the political administration and are responsible for the actual technical implementation of policy. Key functions of the executive include enforcing and implementing laws passed by the legislature, formulating and proposing government policy and the national budget, conducting foreign policy and representing the state in its dealings with other states, serving as commander in chief of the armed forces, and appointing key government officials, judges and ambassadors.\n\nThe judiciary is the organ of government responsible for interpreting the laws made by the legislature, applying these laws to specific cases brought before it, resolving disputes between individuals, between individuals and the state, and between different levels or arms of government, and ensuring that both citizens and the government itself act within the bounds of the law. The judiciary is structured as a hierarchy of courts, typically including lower courts (such as Magistrate Courts, which handle less serious cases), higher courts (such as High Courts, which handle more serious civil and criminal matters), appellate courts (such as the Court of Appeal, which reviews decisions from lower courts), and a supreme court (the highest court in the land, whose decisions are final and binding, and which in many countries also has the power of judicial review to determine whether laws passed by the legislature or actions taken by the executive are constitutional). For the judiciary to perform its functions effectively and fairly, it must possess judicial independence, meaning judges must be free from undue interference or pressure from the legislature, the executive, or any other external party, in order to render impartial judgments based purely on the law and the facts before them, without fear of political retaliation or favour.\n\nSample Exam-Style Questions:\n1. Question: Distinguish between a unicameral and a bicameral legislature, and state one example of each. Answer: A unicameral legislature consists of only a single chamber or house, an example being the National Assembly of many smaller countries. A bicameral legislature consists of two chambers, typically an upper house and a lower house, an example being the Nigerian National Assembly, which comprises the Senate (upper house) and the House of Representatives (lower house).\n2. Question: State four functions of the legislature beyond simply making laws. Answer: The legislature represents the interests of the citizens who elected its members; it approves the national budget and controls public expenditure; it exercises oversight over the activities of the executive to ensure accountability; and it ratifies treaties and important international agreements entered into by the executive arm.\n3. Question: Distinguish between the political executive and the permanent executive, giving an example of each. Answer: The political executive comprises elected or politically appointed officials, such as the President and Ministers, who are responsible for determining government policy and typically change when a new administration takes office. The permanent executive comprises career civil servants and bureaucrats, such as Permanent Secretaries, who remain in their positions regardless of political changes and are responsible for the technical implementation of government policy.\n4. Question: Explain the concept of judicial independence and state why it is important. Answer: Judicial independence means that judges are free from undue interference, pressure or control from the legislature, the executive, or any other external party in the performance of their judicial duties. It is important because it enables judges to render impartial, fair judgments based purely on the law and the facts of each case, without fear of political retaliation, ensuring that justice is administered fairly to all parties, including cases involving disputes between citizens and the government itself.\n\nRevision and Summary Points:\n- Government functions are typically divided among three organs.\n- The three organs are the legislature, executive and judiciary.\n- The legislature is primarily responsible for making laws.\n- A unicameral legislature has a single chamber.\n- A bicameral legislature has two chambers, an upper and a lower house.\n- Nigeria National Assembly comprises the Senate and House of Representatives.\n- The legislature represents citizens, approves budgets and exercises oversight.\n- The legislature can ratify treaties and, in some systems, impeach officials.\n- The executive implements and enforces laws made by the legislature.\n- The executive is headed by a President or Prime Minister.\n- The political executive comprises elected or appointed policy-making officials.\n- The permanent executive comprises career civil servants implementing policy.\n- The executive formulates policy, conducts foreign affairs and commands the armed forces.\n- The judiciary interprets laws and resolves disputes.\n- The judiciary hierarchy includes Magistrate Courts, High Courts and appellate courts.\n- The Supreme Court is the highest court with final and binding decisions.\n- Judicial review allows courts to assess the constitutionality of laws and actions.\n- Judicial independence ensures judges are free from external interference.\n- Judicial independence is essential for fair, impartial justice.\n- Each organ of government has distinct but interrelated functions.\n- Effective government requires cooperation and balance among the three organs.\n- Overlap or conflict between organs can create governance challenges.\n- The doctrine of separation of powers is built on this three-organ structure.\n- Citizens interact with all three organs in different ways in daily life.\n- Elections typically determine membership of the legislature and political executive.\n- Judges are typically appointed rather than elected, to preserve independence.\n- Accountability mechanisms exist to check the power of each organ.\n- The structure of these organs can vary between presidential and parliamentary systems.\n- Understanding these organs is fundamental to understanding how a state functions.\n- A well-functioning government requires each organ to perform its role effectively.',
    'source': 'AI-generated study notes',
